{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-333793" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=6x(t)+2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives an equation of the line in the phase space that contains all equilibrium points of the system above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ed0c4c2b27f649db0e06117faf26a11fa1dc0422ff972c3fcc638a892804135"} {"question" "q-333793" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3x+y=0 \\\\ 6x+2y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the second equation is a multiple of the first one. So, we can disregard the second equation. \n\nFrom the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n3x+y=0\\,⇒\\,y=-3x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ -3k\\end{aligned}],\\,k\\in R.\n$$\n\nThis determines the line with the equation $y=-3x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ed0c4c2b27f649db0e06117faf26a11fa1dc0422ff972c3fcc638a892804135"} {"question" "q-333793" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "88f48b4db518575b6840f0181051ed521d1e0414f32c802d186aaa566174f982"} {"question" "q-333793" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ed0c4c2b27f649db0e06117faf26a11fa1dc0422ff972c3fcc638a892804135"} {"question" "q-333793" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=-3x"] ["math" "y=-3x+1"] ["math" "y=-x"] ["math" "y=3x"] ["math" "y=x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ed0c4c2b27f649db0e06117faf26a11fa1dc0422ff972c3fcc638a892804135"} {"question" "q-333793" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=-3x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ed0c4c2b27f649db0e06117faf26a11fa1dc0422ff972c3fcc638a892804135"} {"question" "q-333795" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4x(t)-2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=8x(t)-4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives an equation of the line in the phase space that contains all equilibrium points of the system above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ed0c4c2b27f649db0e06117faf26a11fa1dc0422ff972c3fcc638a892804135"} {"question" "q-333795" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ 8 & -4\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4x-2y=0 \\\\ 8x-4y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the second equation is a multiple of the first one. So, we can disregard the second equation. \n\nFrom the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n4x-2y=0\\,⇒\\,2x-y=0\\,⇒\\,y=2x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ 2k\\end{aligned}],\\,k\\in R.\n$$\n\nThis determines the line with the equation $y=2x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ed0c4c2b27f649db0e06117faf26a11fa1dc0422ff972c3fcc638a892804135"} {"question" "q-333795" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "88f48b4db518575b6840f0181051ed521d1e0414f32c802d186aaa566174f982"} {"question" "q-333795" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ed0c4c2b27f649db0e06117faf26a11fa1dc0422ff972c3fcc638a892804135"} {"question" "q-333795" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=-2x"] ["math" "y=-x"] ["math" "y=2x"] ["math" "y=2x-1"] ["math" "y=x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ed0c4c2b27f649db0e06117faf26a11fa1dc0422ff972c3fcc638a892804135"} {"question" "q-333795" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=2x"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ed0c4c2b27f649db0e06117faf26a11fa1dc0422ff972c3fcc638a892804135"} {"question" "q-333782" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4x(t)+2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives all equilibrium points of the system of linear differential equations above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ed0c4c2b27f649db0e06117faf26a11fa1dc0422ff972c3fcc638a892804135"} {"question" "q-333782" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x+y=0 \\\\ 4x+2y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the second equation is a multiple of the first one:\n\n$$\n2(2x+y)=4x+2y,\n$$\n\nso we can disregard the second equation.\n\nFrom the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n2x+y=0\\,⇒\\,y=-2x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ -2k\\end{aligned}],\\,k\\in R.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ed0c4c2b27f649db0e06117faf26a11fa1dc0422ff972c3fcc638a892804135"} {"question" "q-333782" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "88f48b4db518575b6840f0181051ed521d1e0414f32c802d186aaa566174f982"} {"question" "q-333782" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ed0c4c2b27f649db0e06117faf26a11fa1dc0422ff972c3fcc638a892804135"} {"question" "q-333782" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\text{ only}"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\text{ only}"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2k \\\\ -2k\\end{aligned}],\\,k\\in R"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ -2k\\end{aligned}],\\,k\\in R"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ 2k\\end{aligned}],\\,k\\in R"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ed0c4c2b27f649db0e06117faf26a11fa1dc0422ff972c3fcc638a892804135"} {"question" "q-333782" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ -2k\\end{aligned}],\\,k\\in R"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ed0c4c2b27f649db0e06117faf26a11fa1dc0422ff972c3fcc638a892804135"} {"question" "q-333794" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-x(t)+2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-2x(t)+4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives an equation of the line in the phase space that contains all equilibrium points of the system above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ed0c4c2b27f649db0e06117faf26a11fa1dc0422ff972c3fcc638a892804135"} {"question" "q-333794" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-x+2y=0 \\\\ -2x+4y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the second equation is a multiple of the first one. So, we can disregard the second equation. \n\nFrom the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n-x+2y=0\\,⇒\\,2y=x\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{x}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ \\frac{k}{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,k\\in R.\n$$\n\nThis determines the line with the equation $y=\\frac{x}{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ed0c4c2b27f649db0e06117faf26a11fa1dc0422ff972c3fcc638a892804135"} {"question" "q-333794" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "88f48b4db518575b6840f0181051ed521d1e0414f32c802d186aaa566174f982"} {"question" "q-333794" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ed0c4c2b27f649db0e06117faf26a11fa1dc0422ff972c3fcc638a892804135"} {"question" "q-333794" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=-2x"] ["math" "y=-\\frac{x}{2}"] ["math" "y=2x"] ["math" "y=\\frac{x}{2}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{x}{2}+1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ed0c4c2b27f649db0e06117faf26a11fa1dc0422ff972c3fcc638a892804135"} {"question" "q-333794" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=\\frac{x}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ed0c4c2b27f649db0e06117faf26a11fa1dc0422ff972c3fcc638a892804135"} {"question" "q-333800" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)+4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives an equation of the line in the phase space that contains all equilibrium points of the system above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ed0c4c2b27f649db0e06117faf26a11fa1dc0422ff972c3fcc638a892804135"} {"question" "q-333800" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+2y=0 \\\\ 2x+4y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the second equation is a multiple of the first one. So, we can disregard the second equation. \n\nFrom first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+2y=0\\,⇒\\,2y=-x\\,⇒\\,y=-\\frac{x}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ -\\frac{k}{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,k\\in R.\n$$\n\nThis determines the line with the equation $y=-\\frac{x}{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ed0c4c2b27f649db0e06117faf26a11fa1dc0422ff972c3fcc638a892804135"} {"question" "q-333800" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "88f48b4db518575b6840f0181051ed521d1e0414f32c802d186aaa566174f982"} {"question" "q-333800" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ed0c4c2b27f649db0e06117faf26a11fa1dc0422ff972c3fcc638a892804135"} {"question" "q-333800" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=-2x"] ["math" "y=-\\frac{x}{2}"] ["math" "y=-\\frac{x}{2}+1"] ["math" "y=2x"] ["math" "y=\\frac{x}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ed0c4c2b27f649db0e06117faf26a11fa1dc0422ff972c3fcc638a892804135"} {"question" "q-333800" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=-\\frac{x}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ed0c4c2b27f649db0e06117faf26a11fa1dc0422ff972c3fcc638a892804135"} {"question" "q-332864" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)+2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[-1,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[1,\\text{ }2]^{T},$ respectively. Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ed0c4c2b27f649db0e06117faf26a11fa1dc0422ff972c3fcc638a892804135"} {"question" "q-332864" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nLet's now sketch the phase portrait.\n\n- First, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ are the equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=-x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/bdfe2e3f89db20665d2c7c1e49c7ddae42387fc1e5bdde8849a9ed1de7e33687.png)\n\n- Now, the line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n$y=2x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. Since ${e}^{3t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/80798b43ad4d439adbe0bdc827f5e1fed64de0a63660c7bca0b610b49445d859.png)\n\nAll other non-equilibrium solutions are parallel to the vector ${v}_{2}.$ So, the phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/690295c0ea992de3c2a484c377ddeb0750f8438d16a30681eb6f8cc81b484946.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ed0c4c2b27f649db0e06117faf26a11fa1dc0422ff972c3fcc638a892804135"} {"question" "q-332864" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "88f48b4db518575b6840f0181051ed521d1e0414f32c802d186aaa566174f982"} {"question" "q-332864" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ed0c4c2b27f649db0e06117faf26a11fa1dc0422ff972c3fcc638a892804135"} {"question" "q-332864" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/1bf19c8cc89bd745c5515570921e1db00b552d351459ccd2e17921d63c4fa3d7.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/2e18d0b6def07666f9eaf4f4d54